anchester United</span><span>, with Romano relaying the club line verbatim &mdash; &ldquo;we want the player to stay&rdquo; &mdash; and by 11 August his own assessment was that the deal &ldquo;looks impossible.&rdquo; They have also made an early enquiry about </span><span style="font-weight:700">Felix Nmecha</span><span>&nbsp;at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Borussia Dortmund</span><span>, where the answer was emphatic. Sporting director </span><span style="font-weight:700">Nils-Ole Book</span><span>: </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;I would consider that highly unlikely&hellip; in Felix&rsquo;s case, I can pretty safely rule it out this summer.&rdquo;</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;Dortmund&rsquo;s asking price is reported at &euro;100&ndash;120m; an &euro;80m release clause activates in the summer of 2027. Status: rumour, publicly denied.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>One coda worth marking: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Pierre-Emile H&oslash;jbjerg</span><span>&nbsp;was, by the account of </span><span style="font-weight:700">Foot Mercato</span><span>, hours from joining Newcastle from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Marseille</span><span>&nbsp;for around &euro;15m when he categorically refused. A source close to owner </span><span style="font-weight:700">Frank McCourt</span><span>&nbsp;said McCourt &ldquo;ne cache pas son profond m&eacute;contentement face &agrave; certaines attitudes&rdquo; and warned that if the club did not act, &ldquo;les cons&eacute;quences pourraient &ecirc;tre extr&ecirc;mement s&eacute;rieuses.&rdquo; Talks are dead. Newcastle open the season at home to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Liverpool</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;on 23 August.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Barcelona are selling to buy &mdash; and Atl&eacute;tico have gone to FIFA</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">Ara&uacute;jo to Anfield, Ferran to Paris, and a formal complaint filed in Zurich over the way the Juli&aacute;n &Aacute;lvarez pursuit has been conducted.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Liverpool</span><span>&nbsp;announced on </span><span style="font-weight:700">10 August</span><span>&nbsp;that they had agreed a deal to sign </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ronald Ara&uacute;jo</span><span>&nbsp;on a season-long loan from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Barcelona</span><span>, subject to a work permit and international clearance; he takes the No. 33. The structure matters more than the headline. Per </span><span style="font-weight:700">El Desmarque</span><span>&nbsp;and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Romano</span><span>, Liverpool cover 100 per cent of a salary reported at around &euro;16m gross (&euro;11m net), and there is a </span><span style="font-weight:700">non-obligatory</span><span>&nbsp;purchase option set at &euro;55m &mdash; a figure ESPN put in a headline and Liverpool have never disclosed. Ara&uacute;jo, whose Barcelona contract runs to 2031 and who was absent for weeks in December and January with documented mental-health difficulties, said: </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;I&rsquo;m super-happy to be here and excited to get started. I&rsquo;m glad about the interest and it was the right move at the right time.&rdquo;</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;Status: official.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The second sale is nearly done. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ferran Torres</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; the man who scored the winning goal in the World Cup final &mdash; is on the point of joining </span><span style="font-weight:700">Paris Saint-Germain</span><span>. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Foot Mercato</span><span>&nbsp;reported an &ldquo;accord de principe&rdquo; between the clubs on 11 August at &euro;50m fixed plus &euro;5m in bonuses, PSG having opened at &euro;40m; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Romano</span><span>&nbsp;the same day had it at &euro;50&ndash;55m with negotiations &ldquo;entering final stages.&rdquo; On </span><span style="font-weight:700">12 August</span><span>, per </span><span style="font-weight:700">Mundo Deportivo</span><span>, Barcelona granted Torres permission to skip training in order to push the move through. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Luis Enrique</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;is reported to have phoned him personally to guarantee minutes in rotation. He scored 21 in 49 last season. Status: fee agreed, personal terms pending, not official.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Why the fire sale? Because LaLiga restored Barcelona to the 1:1 spending rule for this summer &mdash; driven by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Robert Lewandowski</span><span>&rsquo;s wage coming off the books, by Spotify Camp Nou revenues running above projection in the first full season back, and by record VIP and seat-licence billing. But 1:1 is relief, not licence. To fund </span><span style="font-weight:700">Rodri</span><span>&nbsp;at &euro;60m-plus, plus a striker, plus </span><span style="font-weight:700">Jo&atilde;o Cancelo</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Al Hilal</span><span>&nbsp;(agreement with the player at around &euro;10m; no agreement with the club), Barcelona need the wages off and the cash in. </span><span style="font-weight:700">El Desmarque</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;put the three-target bill at &euro;230m on 11 August.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>And then there is the fight. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Atl&eacute;tico Madrid</span><span>&nbsp;have filed a formal complaint with </span><span style="font-weight:700">FIFA</span><span>&nbsp;against Barcelona over the pursuit of </span><span style="font-weight:700">Juli&aacute;n &aacute;lvarez</span><span>, alleging negotiation with a contracted player during a protected period &mdash; the complaint attributed to chief executive </span><span style="font-weight:700">Miguel &aacute;ngel Gil Mar&iacute;n</span><span>. Barcelona&rsquo;s reported offer of &euro;100&ndash;130m expired with a 31 July deadline set by president </span><span style="font-weight:700">Joan Laporta</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">; Atl&eacute;tico point to a &euro;500m release clause and have publicly refused to negotiate, having already turned down a reported &euro;150m from Real Madrid. &aacute;lvarez himself said in July that &ldquo;lo mejor para todos es una transferencia.&rdquo; He was due back at Atl&eacute;tico&rsquo;s pre-season on 10 August. Status: stalled &mdash; and now a legal matter. Single-sourced on the FIFA filing; treat with care.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Dortmund have bid four times for Said El Mala. K&ouml;ln keep saying no.</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">A nineteen-year-old winger, a &euro;55m package, and a selling club with no financial reason to sell. The Bundesliga&rsquo;s defining negotiation.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The ladder, as reported by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Patrick Berger</span><span>&nbsp;of </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sky Deutschland</span><span>&nbsp;and carried by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Get German Football News</span><span>: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Borussia Dortmund</span><span>&rsquo;s first bid for </span><span style="font-weight:700">Said El Mala</span><span>&nbsp;was rejected; a second at &euro;37m guaranteed plus &euro;8m in bonuses was rejected; a third of around &euro;50m with achievable bonuses was rejected on 8 August; and on </span><span style="font-weight:700">12 August</span><span>&nbsp;a fourth arrived at </span><span style="font-weight:700">&euro;50m guaranteed plus &euro;5m in bonuses</span><span>. </span><span style="font-weight:700">1. FC K&ouml;ln</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;want &euro;50m as a fixed fee and, crucially, have &ldquo;little financial necessity to sell.&rdquo;</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>El Mala &mdash; nineteen, thirteen goals and five assists last season &mdash; is willing. Dortmund&rsquo;s internal backing is total: sporting director </span><span style="font-weight:700">Nils-Ole Book</span><span>, managing director </span><span style="font-weight:700">Lars Ricken</span><span>, chief executive </span><span style="font-weight:700">Carsten Cramer</span><span>&nbsp;and head coach </span><span style="font-weight:700">Niko Kova&#269;</span><span>, who has spoken to the player personally. </span><span style="font-weight:700">AC Milan</span><span>&nbsp;are monitoring and could enter if Dortmund fail. Dortmund&rsquo;s fallback is </span><span style="font-weight:700">Giannis Konstantelias</span><span>&nbsp;of </span><span style="font-weight:700">PAOK</span><span>. One caveat this paper will print rather than smooth over: </span><span style="font-weight:700">90min.de</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">, on the same day, reported that no written offer has yet been submitted at all. Status: advanced talks; the reporting conflicts on whether a formal bid exists.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span>Dortmund have already spent this window. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Konstantinos Karetsas</span><span>, an eighteen-year-old attacking midfielder, arrived from </span><span style="font-weight:700">KRC Genk</span><span>&nbsp;on 3 August for &euro;30m guaranteed plus &euro;2m in bonuses, with Genk retaining a 10 per cent sell-on, on a five-year deal to 2031. Ricken: </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;With Kos, we&rsquo;re picking up a player who, at just 18 years of age, already possesses tremendous ability.&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;Set that against </span><span style="font-weight:700">Bayern</span><span>&rsquo;s summer &mdash; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Nathaniel Brown</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Eintracht Frankfurt</span><span>&nbsp;for a Sky-reported &euro;55m and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ismael Saibari</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">PSV</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;for a Dutch club record (&euro;50m or &euro;55m, depending on the outlet) &mdash; and the shape of the German window is two clubs buying and sixteen watching.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Italy&rsquo;s domino week: Frattesi, Spence, Piccoli &mdash; and Lukaku says goodbye</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">Serie A does not open until 22 August, but the market moved harder here than anywhere in Europe this week.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The one confirmed departure of real weight: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Romelu Lukaku</span><span>&nbsp;left </span><span style="font-weight:700">Napoli</span><span>&nbsp;for </span><span style="font-weight:700">Fenerbah&ccedil;e</span><span>, made official on </span><span style="font-weight:700">12 August</span><span>. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Corriere dello Sport</span><span>&nbsp;gives the most granular structure &mdash; &euro;6m plus &euro;2m in bonuses, with a further &euro;1m if Fenerbah&ccedil;e win the Turkish title &mdash; on a one-year contract at a salary slightly below the &euro;11m gross he was on in Naples; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sportmediaset</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;reported a flat &euro;7m. Napoli&rsquo;s farewell noted 45 appearances, 15 goals, a Scudetto and a Supercoppa. Fenerbah&ccedil;e&rsquo;s post read: &ldquo;Big Rom, bigger dreams.&rdquo;</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Inter</span><span>&nbsp;are the fulcrum of everything else. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Davide Frattesi</span><span>&nbsp;is going to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Lazio</span><span>&nbsp;on a &euro;1m loan with a &euro;14m obligation to buy on easy conditions and a further &euro;3&ndash;4m in bonuses &mdash; and, per </span><span style="font-weight:700">Gianluca Di Marzio</span><span>, Inter retain 50 per cent of any future sale. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Luis Henrique</span><span>&nbsp;goes the other way in the capital, to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Roma</span><span>, at a base agreement of around &euro;30m plus bonuses. Coming in: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Djed Spence</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Tottenham</span><span>, described by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Football Italia</span><span>&nbsp;on 12 August as &euro;31.5m plus bonus on a deal to June 2031 (</span><span style="font-weight:700">Corriere</span><span>&nbsp;has &euro;30m plus &euro;5m plus a 10 per cent sell-on), with a medical scheduled for 13 August. Inter must clear a non-EU roster slot to register him. Their first choice in midfield, should Frattesi leave, is reported to be </span><span style="font-weight:700">Curtis Jones</span><span>&nbsp;of </span><span style="font-weight:700">Liverpool</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">. Status: Frattesi and Spence fee-agreed; Jones concrete interest.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Elsewhere: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Nahuel Molina</span><span>&nbsp;completed a move from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Atl&eacute;tico Madrid</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Roma</span><span>&nbsp;on </span><span style="font-weight:700">12 August</span><span>&nbsp;at &euro;13m plus &euro;4m in bonuses. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Trevoh Chalobah</span><span>&nbsp;joined </span><span style="font-weight:700">Como</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Chelsea</span><span>&nbsp;on 9 August for around &euro;30m p